window.QBANK = {
  subject: "Business Law & Corporate Governance", code: "BLCG",
  modules: {"1":"Corporate Governance","2":"Moral Values & Ethics","3":"The Sale of Goods Act","4":"The Companies Act, 2013","5":"Membership"},
  rows: [
[1,"How is corporate governance defined?",["A voluntary code of ethics for CEOs","The combination of rules, processes or laws by which businesses are operated, regulated or controlled","The annual financial reporting requirement of listed companies","The charter granted by a monarch to a company"],1,"Corporate governance is defined as the combination of rules, processes or laws by which businesses are operated, regulated or controlled, encompassing internal and external factors affecting stakeholders.","Ebook","Other options: CSR is NOT the same as corporate governance · The board creates the framework, but governance covers all rules/processes · Governance includes external factors — not just internal policy"],
[1,"Which body is responsible for creating the corporate governance framework of a company?",["CEO and CFO","The audit committee","The board of directors","The Securities and Exchange Commission"],2,"The board of directors is responsible for creating the corporate governance framework that best aligns business conduct with objectives.","Ebook","Other options: The board creates the framework; SEC/CAC adopts the code · Audit committee is a subset of the board, not the primary body · CEO/CFO are executive; governance framework is board-level"],
[1,"Which of the following is NOT one of the five common issues in corporate governance?",["Conflicts of interest","Transparency","Dividend declaration","Ethics violations"],2,"The five issues are: Conflicts of Interest, Oversight Issues, Accountability Issues, Transparency, and Ethics Violations. Dividend declaration is not one of them.","Ebook","Other options: Oversight issues ARE one of the five · Accountability IS one of the five · Ethics violations IS one of the five"],
[1,"Which two corporate scandals are cited in the ebook as examples that triggered the need for corporate governance in India?",["WorldCom and Lehman Brothers","Enron (USA) and Satyam (India)","BCCI and Adelphia","Kingfisher and Jet Airways"],1,"The ebook specifically cites Enron in the USA and Satyam in India as examples of corporate collapses due to bad governance and corrupt practices.","Ebook","Other options: WorldCom is a US fraud but not mentioned in this ebook · Kingfisher/Jet are Indian airline failures, not governance scandals in this context · BCCI is a banking scandal, not cited here"],
[1,"The Code of Best Practices for Corporate Governance was adopted by which two bodies?",["SEBI and Ministry of Finance","Securities and Exchange Commission and Corporate Affairs Commission","RBI and IRDA","Ministry of Law and the Supreme Court"],1,"The Code was adopted by the boards of the Securities and Exchange Commission (capital market regulator) and the Corporate Affairs Commission (company regulator).","Ebook","Other options: SEBI is the Indian equivalent but the ebook text references SEC and CAC · RBI regulates banking, not general corporate governance · Ministry of Finance does not adopt governance codes"],
[1,"According to the Code of Best Practices, for how long can non-executive directors be appointed without shareholder approval?",["One year","Two years","Three years","Five years"],2,"Non-executive directors must be appointed for periods not exceeding three years, except with the approval of the company's shareholders.","Ebook","Other options: Two years is a common distractor but the code says three years · Reappointment is based exclusively on performance, not automatic · Five years applies to certain statutory appointments, not this code"],
[1,"What percentage of issued capital triggers the right of shareholders to board representation under the Code?",["10%","15%","20%","25%"],2,"Shareholders holding more than 20% of the company's issued capital must, where possible, be represented on the board.","Ebook","Other options: 10% is below the threshold · 25% is a common distractor but the code specifies >20% · The code also mandates at least one director for minority shareholders"],
[1,"The remuneration committee under the Code must be composed of:",["Only executive directors","Only independent external auditors","Wholly or mainly non-executive directors","Equal number of executive and non-executive directors"],2,"The remuneration committee must be composed wholly or mainly of non-executive directors, to ensure independence in setting executive pay.","Ebook","Other options: Executive directors cannot set their own remuneration — conflict of interest · External auditors are on the audit committee, not remuneration committee · Equal composition does not guarantee independence"],
[1,"Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) is also known as:",["Corporate legal liability","Corporate citizenship","Corporate disclosure","Corporate audit"],1,"CSR is also called corporate citizenship — companies are conscious of their impact on society, environment, and economy.","Ebook","Other options: Corporate disclosure is a separate governance concept · Corporate legal liability relates to lawsuits, not CSR · Corporate audit relates to financial verification"],
[1,"According to the ebook, three higher purposes of Corporate Social Reporting were identified by:",["Haralambos and Mukerjee","Deegan and Juhman","Guthrie and Parker, 1990","Healy and Palepu, 2001"],2,"Guthrie and Parker (1990) identified three higher purposes of Corporate Social Reporting: comprehensive organizational view, constraining irresponsible behavior, and positive motivation for responsible behavior.","Ebook","Other options: Healy and Palepu cited the aim of corporate disclosure, not CSR reporting purposes · Deegan cited CSR reporting generally, not the three purposes · Haralambos and Mukerjee defined values, not CSR reporting"],
[1,"Corporate disclosure is best defined as:",["Annual audit of financial statements","Communication of information by people inside public firms towards people outside","Monthly reporting to regulators","The process of paying dividends to shareholders"],1,"Corporate disclosure is defined as the communication of information by people inside public firms towards people outside — it is a legal requirement.","Ebook","Other options: Disclosure is NOT just annual — it can be continuous · Dividends are a financial decision, not disclosure per se · Audits verify information; disclosure communicates it"],
[1,"Which section of the Companies Act, 2013 deals with criminal liability for misstatement in a prospectus?",["Section 73","Section 34","Section 36","Section 125"],1,"Section 34 deals with criminal liability for misstatement in the prospectus issued by a company.","Ebook","Other options: Section 73 restricts deposit acceptance from the public · Section 36 deals with fraudulently inducing investment · Section 125 establishes the investor education and protection fund"],
[1,"Under which section must dividend be credited to investor's account after declaration?",["Section 34","Section 73","Section 123","Section 136"],2,"Section 123 states that dividend should be credited to investors' accounts within five days of declaration.","Ebook","Other options: Section 136 gives the right to demand financial statements · Section 73 restricts public deposits · Five days is the specific deadline — not seven or thirty days"],
[1,"The Indian Contract Act, 1872 defines a contract as:",["An oral promise between two parties","An agreement enforceable by law","A written document signed by witnesses","A promise supported by consideration only"],1,"The Indian Contract Act, 1872 defines a contract as 'an agreement enforceable by law.' Thus: Contract = Agreement + Enforceability.","Ebook","Other options: A contract need not be written — it can be oral or implied · Consideration is one element but not the complete definition · Witnesses are required only in specific cases (e.g., immovable property)"],
[1,"Which element of a valid contract requires that parties must agree to the same thing in the same sense at the same time?",["Consideration","Consent","Contractual capacity","Certainty"],1,"Consent requires parties to agree upon the same thing in the same sense simultaneously. An agreement without consent is not legally binding.","Ebook","Other options: Contractual capacity refers to legal competence (age, sanity) · Consideration means something in return (cash, kind, act) · Certainty means terms must not be vague"],
[1,"A void agreement under the Indian Contract Act is defined in which section?",["Section 10","Section 2(g)","Section 27","Section 68"],1,"Section 2(g) defines a void agreement as 'an agreement not enforceable by law.'","Ebook","Other options: Section 10 states conditions for a valid contract · Section 27 deals with performance of contracts · Section 68 covers quasi-contracts (necessaries to incapable persons)"],
[1,"Performance of a contract requires the performance to be:",["Approximate and commercial","Exact and complete","Partial is acceptable with notice","Optional if both parties agree verbally"],1,"For performance to be effective, courts expect it to be exact and complete — matching the contractual obligations precisely.","Ebook","Other options: Partial performance only applies in specific severable contracts · Approximate performance may lead to breach · Verbal agreement to reduce performance may not be enforceable"],
[1,"Under Section 73 of the Indian Contract Act, compensation for breach of contract covers:",["All losses including remote and indirect losses","Only loss that naturally arose in the usual course or was foreseeable by both parties","Only the loss of profit","Only the cost of litigation"],1,"Section 73 entitles compensation for loss that naturally arose in the usual course of things from the breach, or which the parties knew when making the contract would result — NOT remote or indirect losses.","Ebook","Other options: Remote and indirect losses are explicitly excluded · Loss of profit may or may not be recoverable depending on foreseeability · Section 73 does NOT cover litigation costs automatically"],
[1,"The word 'Quasi' in Quasi contract means:",["Legal","Partial","Pseudo/False","Voluntary"],2,"'Quasi' means pseudo. A Quasi contract is a pseudo-contract — not a real contract, but recognized by courts as resembling one and enforced like one.","Ebook","Other options: Quasi contracts lack the basic elements of offer and acceptance · They are imposed by law, NOT formed by agreement · They are NOT void — they are enforced by courts"],
[1,"Which sections of the Indian Contract Act cover Quasi contracts?",["Sections 1–5","Sections 27–32","Sections 68–72","Sections 90–95"],2,"Sections 68–72 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872 detail five circumstances under which a Quasi contract comes to exist.","Ebook","Other options: Section 27 deals with performance, not quasi-contracts · Sections 1–5 are definitions and preliminary · Sections 90–95 are not in the ICA quasi-contract coverage"],
[1,"Under Section 69 of the Indian Contract Act (Quasi contract), who is entitled to reimbursement?",["A finder of goods","A person who pays on someone else's behalf the money the other person was bound by law to pay","A supplier of necessaries to a minor","A person enjoying a non-gratuitous act"],1,"Section 69 covers payment by an interested person — if you pay money that another person was legally bound to pay, you are entitled to reimbursement from that person.","Ebook","Other options: Section 68 covers necessaries to incapable persons · Section 70 covers obligation of person enjoying non-gratuitous act · Section 71 covers responsibility of finder of goods"],
[1,"According to the ebook, the main aim of corporate disclosure as stated by Healy and Palepu (2001) is:",["To reduce taxation","To communicate firm performance and governance to outside investors","To attract foreign direct investment","To comply with audit requirements"],1,"Healy and Palepu (2001) stated that the main aim of corporate disclosure is 'to communicate firm performance and governance to outside investors.'","Ebook","Other options: Corporate disclosure improves capital allocation efficiency as a secondary benefit · It reduces information gap between firm and stakeholders · Tax reduction is not the aim of disclosure"],
[1,"The concept of corporate social and environmental responsibility was first introduced in the 1990s by:",["Indian PSUs","Multinational companies","Reserve Bank of India","SEBI-listed companies"],1,"According to Juhman (2014) cited in the ebook, the concept of corporate social and environmental responsibility was first introduced in the 1990s by multinational companies.","Ebook","Other options: The concept evolved in the 1970s–1980s in academia but was formally introduced by MNCs in the 1990s · Indian PSUs were not the originators · SEBI guidelines on CSR came later (Companies Act 2013)"],
[1,"Which historical event is cited in the ebook as increasing awareness of corporate environmental responsibility?",["2008 global financial crisis","Union Carbide Gas Leak and Exxon Valdez oil spill","Satyam scandal","Enron collapse"],1,"Historical events like the Exxon Valdez oil spill in Alaska (Patten, 1992) and Union Carbide Gas Leak (Blackoconiere & Pattern, 1994) in India increased corporate environmental responsibility awareness.","Ebook","Other options: Satyam and Enron are governance scandals, not environmental events · 2008 financial crisis triggered banking governance reforms · These environmental events are specifically cited in the CSR reporting section"],
[2,"According to M. Haralambos (2000), a value is defined as:",["A financial asset of a company","A belief that something is good and desirable","A legal obligation to act ethically","A social norm enforced by government"],1,"Haralambos (2000) defined a value as 'a belief that something is good and desirable' — a foundational definition in the module.","Ebook","Other options: R.K. Mukerjee's definition focuses on socially approved desires and goals (not Haralambos) · Values are NOT financial assets · Values differ from legal norms — they are internalized beliefs"],
[2,"R.K. Mukerjee (1949) is described in the ebook as:",["A management guru from the USA","A pioneer Indian sociologist who initiated the study of social values","A British economist who studied corporate governance","The founder of the BRICS framework"],1,"R.K. Mukerjee (1949) is described as a pioneer Indian sociologist who initiated the study of social values.","Ebook","Other options: Haralambos is the other values theorist mentioned — but he is not Indian · BRICS was coined by Goldman Sachs economists, not Mukerjee · Mukerjee's 1949 definition precedes most management theory"],
[2,"Intrinsic values are also known as:",["Instrumental values","Proximate values","Ultimate and transcendent values","Organizational values"],2,"Intrinsic values are also known as ultimate and transcendent values — they relate to goals of life and occupy the highest place in the hierarchy of values.","Ebook","Other options: Instrumental values are the MEANS to achieve intrinsic values · Proximate values = instrumental values (another name for the lower level) · Organizational values are a separate classification"],
[2,"Which type of values are means to achieve the goals of life (intrinsic values)?",["Ultimate values","Transcendent values","Instrumental values","Spiritual values"],2,"Instrumental values (also called incidental or proximate values) are the means to achieve the goals (intrinsic values) of life.","Ebook","Other options: Ultimate/transcendent values = intrinsic values, not the means · Spiritual values are a separate category discussed later in the module · Instrumental comes after intrinsic in the value hierarchy"],
[2,"Value formation is described as the confluence of:",["Education and government policy","Personal experiences and particular culture","Religious beliefs and economic status","Genetic factors and peer pressure"],1,"Value formation is the confluence of our personal experiences and the particular culture we are entwined in. Values are imposed from family in childhood and reinforced through culture.","Ebook","Other options: Education contributes but is not the complete explanation · Religion may influence values but is not cited as the primary factor · Genetics are not discussed in the ebook's treatment of value formation"],
[2,"Which of the following is NOT listed among the 12 values of Indian Managers in the ebook?",["Integrity","Willingness to take Risk","Profitability maximization","Emotional Intelligence"],2,"The 12 values listed are Integrity, Risk-taking, Optimism, Commitment to Growth, Vision, Pragmatism, Responsibility, Hard Work, Self-confidence, Emotional Intelligence, Expertise, and Ability to Engage Others. Profitability maximization is not among them.","Ebook","Other options: Integrity IS value #1 · Emotional Intelligence IS value #10 · Hard Work and Conscientiousness IS value #8"],
[2,"'Analysis paralysis' is mentioned in the context of which value of Indian Managers?",["Vision","Pragmatism","Willingness to take Risk","Self-confidence"],2,"Willingness to take Risk: leaders avoid 'analysis paralysis' — they take calculated risks without over-analyzing.","Ebook","Other options: Vision relates to clear planning and communication, not risk paralysis · Pragmatism is about being realistic and grounded · Self-confidence relates to inner strength and not needing approval"],
[2,"Management Ethics is defined in the ebook as:",["Maximizing shareholder returns through ethical investments","The discipline dealing with what is good and bad, or right and wrong, or with moral duty and obligation","The legal compliance framework for corporate directors","The audit process for ethical behavior in organizations"],1,"Management Ethics is defined as 'the discipline dealing with what is good and bad, or right and wrong, or with moral duty and obligation — a standard of behavior that guides individual managers in their works.'","Ebook","Other options: Business ethics is application of ethical principles — closely related but definition differs · Legal compliance is part of ethics but not the full definition · Shareholder returns are a business objective, not an ethics definition"],
[2,"According to John Donaldson, Business Ethics is:",["The annual sustainability report of a company","The systematic study of ethical matters pertaining to business, industry or related activities, institutions and beliefs","A government-mandated code of conduct for directors","The process of aligning profit with social welfare"],1,"According to John Donaldson, Business Ethics is 'the systematic study of ethical matters pertaining to business industry or related activities, institutions and beliefs' — and the systematic handling of values in business and industry.","Ebook","Other options: Donaldson's definition is about systematic STUDY, not just a code · Sustainability reports are a CSR output, not the definition of business ethics · It is not government-mandated according to Donaldson"],
[2,"A structural business ethics problem arises when:",["Companies exceed their annual budget","Shareholder wealth maximization excludes other stakeholders, creating an imbalance in benefits and obligations","Government imposes new tax regulations","A company fails to publish its annual report"],1,"A structural business ethics problem persists when shareholder wealth maximization excludes other stakeholders who must assume obligations, creating a long-term imbalance in benefits received versus obligations undertaken.","Ebook","Other options: Budget overruns are financial issues, not structural ethics problems · Tax regulations are compliance issues · Annual report failures are governance/disclosure issues, not structural ethics"],
[2,"BRICS stands for:",["Brazil, Russia, India, China, South Africa","Britain, Russia, Indonesia, Canada, Saudi Arabia","Brazil, Romania, India, China, Spain","Brazil, Russia, Iran, China, South Africa"],0,"BRICS is an acronym for Brazil, Russia, India, China and South Africa — the world's leading emerging economies.","Ebook","Other options: South Africa, NOT Saudi Arabia is the 'S' in BRICS · Indonesia is not a BRICS member · Spain is not a BRICS member"],
[2,"According to Goldman Sachs projections cited in the ebook, by 2050 the BRIC economies will:",["Lag behind the G7 economies","Be the most important economies, relegating the US to fifth place","Merge into a single economic bloc","Surpass the EU combined GDP only"],1,"Goldman Sachs believed that by 2050, the BRIC economies would be the most important economies, relegating the US to fifth place.","Ebook","Other options: The projection suggests BRIC dominance, not lagging · They are currently an investment category, not a single bloc · The US to 5th place is the specific claim — not 3rd or 4th"],
[2,"According to the ebook, ESG stands for:",["Economic, Social and Governance","Environmental, Social and Governance","Ethical, Sustainability and Growth","Environmental, Stakeholder and Governance"],1,"ESG stands for Environmental, Social, and Governance — metrics used by investors to analyze an organization's ethical impact and sustainability practices.","Ebook","Other options: Economic is NOT the E in ESG — it's Environmental · Stakeholder is not the S — it's Social · ESG is used for investor analysis, not just regulatory compliance"],
[2,"The Starbucks example in the ebook illustrates:",["Managerial values and integrity","Corporate Social Responsibility in practice","Business ethics dilemmas","Stakeholder management failures"],1,"Starbucks is given as a CSR example — 99% ethically sourced coffee, green building, community service, college program for employees — before its 1992 IPO.","Ebook","Other options: Starbucks' IPO was in 1992 — before which it already had CSR practices · The Starbucks example is under CSR, not managerial values · It demonstrates proactive CSR, not an ethics dilemma"],
[2,"An ethical dilemma is defined as:",["A legal dispute between two companies","A problem in the decision-making process between two possible options, neither of which is absolutely acceptable from an ethical perspective","A situation where one option is clearly right and one is clearly wrong","A regulatory compliance failure"],1,"An ethical dilemma (ethical paradox or moral dilemma) is a problem in the decision-making process between two possible options, neither of which is absolutely acceptable from an ethical perspective.","Ebook","Other options: Simple moral problems have clear right/wrong answers — dilemmas do not · Ethical dilemmas are NOT legal disputes · The key word is 'neither' — both options have ethical issues"],
[2,"The development of ethics in an organization begins with which step according to the ebook?",["Enforcing policies","Being your own change agent","Making your expectations clear by writing them down","Hiring an ethics officer"],2,"Step 1 in developing ethics: Make your expectations clear — teach employees what you mean by ethical behavior by writing down your expectations.","Ebook","Other options: Enforcing policies is Step 2 · Being your own change agent is Step 3 · Hiring an ethics officer is not mentioned in the three steps"],
[2,"Spirituality's role in business ethics, according to the ebook conclusion, shows:",["No relationship with ethical decision-making","Negative correlation with business performance","Increasing positive consensus on empirically tested relationship between spirituality and ethics","Is relevant only in non-profit organizations"],2,"The ebook concludes: 'there is an increasing positive consensus on empirically tested relationship between spirituality and ethics in the studies.'","Ebook","Other options: The relationship is POSITIVE, not negative · The consensus applies broadly, not just non-profits · The empirical testing is cited — it's not just theoretical"],
[2,"Sustainability in business addresses two main categories — which pair is correct?",["Financial performance and stakeholder management","The effect on environment and the effect on society","Regulatory compliance and shareholder returns","Employee welfare and customer satisfaction"],1,"Sustainability in business addresses: (1) the effect business has on the environment and (2) the effect business has on society.","Ebook","Other options: Financial performance is an outcome, not a sustainability category · Regulatory compliance is governance, not sustainability per se · Employee welfare falls under social sustainability but is not stated as the two main categories"],
[3,"Under the Sale of Goods Act, when the property in goods is transferred from seller to buyer immediately, it is called:",["Agreement to sell","Conditional sale","Sale","Absolute transfer"],2,"When the property in goods is transferred from the seller to the buyer immediately, the contract is called a 'sale.' When transfer is to take place in future or on a condition, it is an 'agreement to sell.'","Ebook","Other options: Agreement to sell = future transfer or conditional transfer · An 'agreement to sell' BECOMES a 'sale' when conditions are fulfilled · The subject matter of the contract must be goods, not services or immovable property"],
[3,"The subject matter of a contract of sale MUST be:",["Immovable property","Services","Goods (movable property)","Actionable claims"],2,"The subject matter of a contract of sale must be goods — every kind of movable property except actionable claims and money. Services are not covered; immovable property is governed by the Transfer of Property Act.","Ebook","Other options: Immovable property is governed by the Transfer of Property Act · Actionable claims are explicitly EXCLUDED from goods definition · Services contracts are NOT covered by the Sale of Goods Act"],
[3,"'Contingent goods' in a contract of sale means:",["Goods already owned by the seller","Goods to be manufactured in the future","Goods whose sale may or may not take place depending on a contingency","Damaged goods sold at reduced price"],2,"Contingent goods are those where the sale may or may not take place — their acquisition by the seller depends upon a contingency which may or may not happen.","Ebook","Other options: Existing goods are already owned by the seller · Future goods will be ready for transfer after a certain time · Contingent goods depend on an uncertain event occurring"],
[3,"A condition in a contract of sale is a stipulation:",["Collateral to the main purpose, breach gives only damages","Essential to the main purpose, breach gives right to repudiate","That can only be waived by the seller","That applies only to future goods"],1,"A condition is a stipulation ESSENTIAL to the main purpose of the contract, the breach of which gives rise to a right to treat the contract as repudiated (rejected/cancelled).","Ebook","Other options: Warranty is collateral to main purpose — breach gives damages ONLY · Breach of condition = right to repudiate + damages · Breach of warranty = damages ONLY, no right to repudiate"],
[3,"A warranty in a contract of sale differs from a condition because:",["Warranty breach gives the right to repudiate; condition does not","Warranty is essential to the main purpose of the contract","Warranty breach gives only a claim for damages, not the right to repudiate","Warranties cannot be waived by the buyer"],2,"A warranty is a stipulation collateral to the main purpose, the breach of which gives rise to a claim for damages but NOT the right to reject goods and repudiate the contract.","Ebook","Other options: Condition breach = right to repudiate; warranty breach = damages only · A condition can be treated as a warranty (buyer's choice to waive) · Whether a stipulation is a condition or warranty depends on contract construction"],
[3,"Section 20 of the Sale of Goods Act covers:",["Unascertained goods and appropriation","Specific goods to be put in deliverable state","Specific goods in a deliverable state (property passes at contract formation)","Ascertainment of price"],2,"Section 20 covers specific goods in a deliverable state — property passes from seller to buyer at the time of formation of the contract (not delivery or payment).","Ebook","Other options: Section 21 covers goods to be PUT into deliverable state · Section 23 covers unascertained goods · Section 9 covers ascertainment of price"],
[3,"In the TV example under Section 21, property passes to the buyer when:",["The contract is signed","Full payment is made","The operating system is installed and buyer notified","The TV is physically delivered to buyer's home"],2,"Under Section 21, where goods must be put in a deliverable state, property passes only after the seller performs the necessary acts (installs OS) and notifies the buyer.","Ebook","Other options: Signing the contract is not sufficient for Section 21 goods · Payment is not the trigger under Section 21 · Delivery alone is insufficient — the notification after putting in deliverable state is required"],
[3,"An 'unpaid seller' is one where:",["The seller has refused to accept payment","The whole price has not been paid or tendered, or a conditional payment instrument has been dishonoured","The seller has delivered defective goods","The buyer has cancelled the order"],1,"An unpaid seller is defined as one where (a) the whole price has not been paid or tendered, or (b) a bill of exchange or negotiable instrument received as conditional payment has not been fulfilled due to dishonour.","Ebook","Other options: An unpaid seller can still have the property passed to the buyer · The seller is unpaid even if he received a cheque that bounced · Defective goods relate to conditions/warranties, not unpaid seller definition"],
[3,"Which of the following is NOT a right of the unpaid seller?",["Lien on goods while in possession","Right of stoppage in transit on buyer's insolvency","Right of resale","Right to claim penalty from the buyer's guarantor"],3,"The three rights of an unpaid seller are: (1) lien on goods, (2) right of stoppage in transit on buyer's insolvency, and (3) right of resale. There is no right to claim penalty from guarantor.","Ebook","Other options: All three lien/stoppage/resale rights are explicitly stated · Stoppage in transit arises specifically on INSOLVENCY of buyer · Right of withholding delivery exists when property hasn't passed"],
[3,"Negotiable instruments are best described as:",["Non-transferable fixed deposits","Written contracts whose benefit could be passed on from original holder to a new holder","Verbal agreements to pay on demand","Documents requiring court enforcement only"],1,"Negotiable instruments are written contracts whose benefit can be passed on from the original holder to a new holder — transferable signed documents promising payment to the bearer.","Ebook","Other options: They ARE transferable — that is their defining feature · Negotiable instruments are NOT verbal — they must be written · Final holder obtains full legal title to the instrument"],
[3,"A promissory note is:",["An unconditional order to a bank to pay","A written promise by an entity to pay a certain sum by a pre-decided date","A certificate of deposit issued by the government","An instrument drawn by the creditor on the debtor"],1,"A promissory note is a written PROMISE by an entity or individual to pay a certain sum by a pre-decided date — showing the amount owed, interest rate, and payment date.","Ebook","Other options: A bill of exchange is an ORDER to pay, not a promise · A cheque is an unconditional order addressed to a banker · Promissory notes show amount, interest rate, and date of payment"],
[3,"When a bill of exchange is issued by a financial institution, it is known as:",["A trade draft","A bank draft","A promissory note","A cheque"],1,"When a bill of exchange is issued by a financial institution, it is called a bank draft. When issued by an individual, it is called a trade draft.","Ebook","Other options: Trade draft = issued by individual · Bank draft = issued by financial institution · A cheque is a separate instrument — unconditional order to a banker by account holder"],
[3,"'Holder in due course' means a person who:",["Holds a negotiable instrument after its maturity","Receives a negotiable instrument as a gift without consideration","Became the possessor for consideration before maturity, without knowledge of defect in title","Holds an instrument after it has been dishonoured"],2,"Holder in due course means any person who for CONSIDERATION became the possessor of a promissory note/bill/cheque BEFORE maturity, WITHOUT sufficient cause to believe any defect existed in the title of the transferor.","Ebook","Other options: Must be BEFORE maturity — not after · Consideration must be present — gift does not qualify · Must NOT have knowledge of title defect"],
[3,"A bill of exchange is dishonoured by non-acceptance if the drawee:",["Pays less than the full amount","Makes default in acceptance within the required time","Refuses a second presentment","Issues a promissory note instead"],1,"A bill of exchange is dishonoured by non-acceptance when the drawee makes default in acceptance upon being required to accept the bill (48 hours are allowed for acceptance).","Ebook","Other options: 48 hours is the specific time allowed for acceptance · Non-payment dishonour is separate from non-acceptance dishonour · Partial payment is not dishonour by non-acceptance"],
[3,"After a negotiable instrument is dishonoured, the holder MUST:",["Immediately file a lawsuit","Give notice of dishonour to all parties against whom he intends to proceed","Accept partial payment","Wait 30 days before taking action"],1,"After dishonour, the holder MUST give notice of dishonour to all parties against whom he intends to proceed. No suit can be filed against a prior party who hasn't been notified.","Ebook","Other options: A lawsuit cannot be filed without first giving notice of dishonour · Notice must be given by the holder OR a party remaining liable · 30 days is not the required waiting period"],
[3,"'Noting' in the context of negotiable instruments refers to:",["Writing a new promissory note","Recording the fact of dishonour by a Notary Public on the instrument","Filing a court case for dishonour","Endorsing the instrument to a new holder"],1,"Noting is the process where a Notary Public records the dishonour on or attached to the instrument, specifying date of dishonour, reason, and notary charges — must be done within reasonable time after dishonour.","Ebook","Other options: Protest is the formal CERTIFICATE issued by notary — noting is just the record · Noting must specify: (1) date of dishonour, (2) reason, (3) notary charges · Noting must be done within reasonable time after dishonour"],
[3,"A key difference between negotiation and assignment of a negotiable instrument is:",["Assignment requires delivery only; negotiation requires a written document","Negotiation requires delivery only; assignment requires a written document signed by transferor","Both require the same formalities","Assignment gives better title than negotiation"],1,"Negotiation requires only delivery (for bearer instruments) to constitute a transfer; assignment requires a written document signed by the transferor.","Ebook","Other options: In assignment, notice must be given to the debtor; in negotiation — no notice needed · Consideration is presumed in negotiation; must be proved in assignment · The assignee takes subject to defects in the assignor's title — negotiation gives better protection"],
[3,"Under which section of the Sale of Goods Act is price ascertainment covered?",["Section 7","Section 9","Section 11","Section 19"],1,"Section 9 covers ascertainment of price — the price may be fixed by contract, by agreed manner, or by course of dealing. If not determined, the buyer pays a reasonable price.","Ebook","Other options: Section 7 covers goods perishing before contract · Section 11 covers stipulations as to time · Section 19 covers when property is intended to pass"],
[3,"Where the price in a contract of sale is not determined, the buyer shall pay:",["The market price at time of delivery","The price listed in the seller's catalogue","A reasonable price","Nothing — the contract is void"],2,"Under Section 9(2), where price is not determined, the buyer shall pay the seller a reasonable price — what is reasonable is a question of fact.","Ebook","Other options: The contract is NOT void just because price is unspecified · Market price at delivery is relevant but not the exact rule · Catalogue price is not mentioned in Section 9"],
[3,"A contract for sale of goods can be made:",["Only in writing with two witnesses","Only through a formal registered document","By offer and acceptance, in writing or by word of mouth or partly both, or implied by conduct","Only through a licensed broker"],2,"A contract of sale may be made in writing, by word of mouth, partly in writing and partly by word of mouth, or may be implied from the conduct of the parties.","Ebook","Other options: No registration is required for a contract of sale (unlike immovable property) · Witnesses are not required · Conduct alone can create a binding contract of sale"],
[3,"If specific goods perish BEFORE the contract of sale is made, the contract is:",["Voidable at buyer's option","Valid but with reduced price","Void","Enforceable with compensation"],2,"Under Section 7, if specific goods have perished (without seller's knowledge) at the time the contract was made, the contract is VOID.","Ebook","Other options: Section 8 covers perishing AFTER agreement to sell but before sale — contract is avoided · Section 7 = perishing before contract = void (never formed) · Section 8 = perishing after agreement to sell = avoided (terminated)"],
[3,"Stipulations as to TIME OF PAYMENT in a contract of sale are generally:",["Always of the essence of the contract","Deemed to be of the essence unless different intention appears","NOT deemed to be of the essence of the contract unless different intention appears","Automatically of the essence for goods above Rs 1 lakh"],2,"Under Section 11, unless a different intention appears from the terms of the contract, stipulations as to TIME OF PAYMENT are NOT deemed to be of the essence of a contract of sale.","Ebook","Other options: Other time stipulations (e.g., delivery date) may or may not be of the essence — depends on terms · Time of payment is specifically NOT of the essence by default · This is a common MCQ trap — payment time ≠ essence"],
[3,"The number of essential elements of a Contract of Sale as listed in the ebook is:",["Four","Five","Six","Seven"],2,"The ebook lists six essential elements: Goods, Price, Two parties, Transfer of ownership, All essentials of a valid contract, and Includes both sale and agreement to sell.","Ebook","Other options: Not four or five — the ebook is specific about six · Both sale and agreement to sell are covered under one umbrella term · Two parties = seller and buyer; one cannot buy one's own goods"],
[3,"Under Section 23(2) of the Sale of Goods Act, property in unascertained goods passes when:",["The buyer signs the contract","The seller delivers to the buyer's premises","The seller unconditionally appropriates goods and delivers to buyer/carrier/bailee without reserving disposal rights","The court issues a delivery order"],2,"Under Section 23(2), if the seller delivers property to the buyer/carrier/bailee for transmission to the buyer WITHOUT reserving disposal rights, he has unconditionally appropriated the property and it passes to the buyer.","Ebook","Other options: Reservation of right of disposal prevents property from passing · Section 23(1) covers appropriation by description with consent · The key word is 'unconditionally' — reservation kills the transfer"],
[4,"According to Chief Justice Marshall of USA, a company is:",["A voluntary association of natural persons for profit","An artificial person, invisible, intangible, existing only in the contemplation of law","A partnership of more than 20 persons","A government-owned enterprise for public benefit"],1,"Chief Justice Marshall described a company as 'a person, artificial, invisible, intangible, and existing only in the contemplation of the law' — possessing only those properties law confers on it.","Ebook","Other options: Haney defined Joint Stock Company as a voluntary association for profit · Lord Lindley focused on contribution of money to common stock · Marshall's definition highlights the artificial/intangible nature"],
[4,"The concept of 'separate legal entity' means:",["The company's assets belong equally to all shareholders","Creditors can sue individual shareholders for company debts","The company is distinct from its members and can own property in its own name","Directors are personally liable for all company losses"],2,"Separate legal entity means the company is legally distinct from its members. Creditors can only recover from company property, not individual shareholders. The company owns property in its own name.","Ebook","Other options: Shareholders do NOT jointly own company assets — the company does · Creditors CANNOT sue individual shareholders (limited liability) · Directors have limited personal liability except in fraud/breach of duty cases"],
[4,"'Perpetual existence' of a company means:",["The company can never be dissolved","The company's existence is not affected by death, insolvency or retirement of any shareholder or director","The company can operate indefinitely without annual reports","Directors hold their positions permanently"],1,"Perpetual existence means the company's life does not depend on the death, insolvency or retirement of shareholders/directors. Law creates it and only law can dissolve it — like a river whose water changes but identity remains.","Ebook","Other options: A company CAN be dissolved — through winding up · Perpetual existence means shareholder changes don't kill the company · Annual reports are still required — perpetual existence doesn't exempt them"],
[4,"The 'common seal' of a company serves as:",["The company's registered trademark","A substitute for the company's signature on legal documents","The emblem of the board of directors","The letterhead used in all official communications"],1,"Since a company is artificial and cannot sign documents, the common seal (with the company's name engraved) acts as its signature. Documents bearing the common seal are legally binding on the company.","Ebook","Other options: Common seal = signature substitute for an artificial entity · Two directors AND the secretary must be present when seal is affixed (Table A Reg 84) · Common seal is different from the company's trademark or logo"],
[4,"Which of the following is an example of a Statutory Company?",["Google India Pvt Ltd","East India Company","Reserve Bank of India","Reliance Industries Ltd"],2,"Statutory companies are incorporated by a special act of Parliament or State legislature. The Reserve Bank of India (formed under RBI Act, 1934) is a classic example. So is LIC (formed under LIC Act, 1956).","Ebook","Other options: East India Company was a Royal Chartered Company · Google India Pvt Ltd is a registered/incorporated company · Reliance Industries is a registered company under the Companies Act"],
[4,"What is the minimum number of members required to form a Public Limited Company?",["2","3","5","7"],3,"A Public Limited Company requires a minimum of 7 members. There is no maximum limit. A private company requires a minimum of 2 members.","Ebook","Other options: 2 = minimum for private company · Public company requires 3 DIRECTORS (not 3 members) · 5 members = quorum for a public company meeting, not minimum formation requirement"],
[4,"A Private Company limits its membership to a maximum of:",["25","50","100","200"],1,"A private company limits membership to 50 (excluding employees who are members or ex-employees who continue to be members). The minimum is 2.","Ebook","Other options: Where two persons hold shares jointly, they count as ONE member · 200 applies to certain other provisions, not the membership limit · Public company has NO maximum member limit"],
[4,"One Person Company (OPC) was introduced in India under:",["Companies Act, 1956","Companies Act, 2013","Companies Amendment Act, 2017","SEBI Act, 1992"],1,"OPC (One Person Company) as a company type was introduced in the Companies Act, 2013 in India. It is similar to sole proprietorship but offers limited liability protection.","Ebook","Other options: Companies Act 1956 did not have OPC · OPC owner has limited liability — personal assets protected · OPC is the most recent addition to company types in India"],
[4,"In a company limited by guarantee, members promise to pay:",["The face value of shares on winding up","A fixed sum specified in the Memorandum in the event of liquidation","The full outstanding debts of the company","Nothing — members have zero liability"],1,"In companies limited by guarantee, members promise to pay a fixed sum (the 'guarantee') specified in the Memorandum in the event of liquidation. This is used mainly by non-profit organizations.","Ebook","Other options: Companies limited by SHARES have liability to the extent of unpaid share value · Unlimited companies have no limit on member liability · Guarantee amount = reserve capital, not called until winding up"],
[4,"A company becomes a subsidiary of another (holding) company when the holding company holds more than:",["25% of equity capital","33% of equity capital","50% of equity capital or voting rights, or can appoint majority of directors","75% of equity capital"],2,"A company becomes a holding company of another if it holds more than 50% of normal value of issued equity capital, OR more than 50% of voting rights, OR secures the right to appoint majority of directors.","Ebook","Other options: 25% gives substantial interest but not control/subsidiary status · 33% is a blocking minority, not majority control · The three alternative conditions are all valid routes to subsidiary status"],
[4,"A Government Company is one where the paid-up capital held by government is:",["More than 25%","At least 33%","Not less than 51%","100%"],2,"A Government Company is one where not less than 51% of the paid-up capital is held by the Central Government, State Government, or both.","Ebook","Other options: 51% = majority, not 100% (partial government ownership still qualifies) · 25% or 33% are not the thresholds for government company status · A government company is not automatically the government's agent"],
[4,"The Memorandum of Association is described as:",["The internal regulation governing daily operations","The charter of the company — defines objects and fundamental conditions of formation","A contract between directors and shareholders","The annual compliance document filed with SEBI"],1,"The Memorandum of Association is the charter of the company. It defines objects, lays down fundamental conditions, governs the company's relationship with the outside world, and defines the scope of activities.","Ebook","Other options: Articles of Association = internal management rules (not MOA) · MOA governs external relationship; AOA governs internal management · An act ultra vires the MOA is void and cannot be ratified"],
[4,"The Memorandum must be subscribed to by at least how many persons for a public company?",["2","3","5","7"],3,"The Memorandum must be subscribed to by at least 7 persons for a public company and at least 2 persons for a private company.","Ebook","Other options: 2 = minimum subscribers for a private company · 5 is not the minimum subscriber requirement · Subscribers to MOA are deemed members on registration"],
[4,"Articles of Association are best described as:",["The external charter of the company defining its objects","Rules, regulations and bye-laws governing the internal affairs of the company","The annual financial statements of the company","The prospectus issued to the public"],1,"Articles of Association are the rules, regulations and bye-laws for governing the internal affairs of the company — the powers of directors, officers, and shareholders.","Ebook","Other options: MOA = external charter; AOA = internal rules · AOA must not contradict the MOA or Companies Act · A public company may adopt Table A in full, in part, or write its own Articles"],
[4,"If a public company's Articles do not exclude Table A provisions, those provisions:",["Are void and unenforceable","Require fresh approval at AGM to apply","Automatically apply to the company","Must be approved by SEBI first"],2,"Unless the Articles of a public company expressly exclude any or all provisions of Table A, those provisions shall automatically apply to the company.","Ebook","Other options: Table A applies by DEFAULT unless expressly excluded · A public company has 3 options: adopt fully, exclude fully, or partial adoption · Private company follows its own Articles or model forms"],
[4,"A prospectus can be issued ONLY by:",["Both public and private companies","A public listed company intending to offer shares/debentures","A private company to its existing shareholders","Any company for rights issue"],1,"Only a public listed company that intends to offer shares or debentures can issue a prospectus. A private company is PROHIBITED from inviting the public and cannot issue a prospectus.","Ebook","Other options: A private company that converts to public may then issue a prospectus · Prospectus must be issued AFTER incorporation — not before · Rights issue to existing shareholders does not require a public prospectus"],
[4,"CRISIL in the context of a prospectus stands for:",["Credit Rating Information Services of India Limited","Central Registry for Issuing Securities in India Limited","Company Registration and Insolvency Services of India","Credit Risk Insurance Services in India Limited"],0,"CRISIL stands for Credit Rating Information Services of India Limited — the credit rating agency whose rating is included in the prospectus content.","Ebook","Other options: CRISIL provides credit rating, not insurance · CRISIL is mentioned under 'Content of Prospectus' in the Study Guide · Credit rating from CRISIL is one of the mandatory disclosures in a prospectus"],
[4,"Under Section 46 of the Companies Act, a share certificate is:",["Conclusive proof of ownership","Prima facie evidence of title","A government-issued document","A transfer deed"],1,"Section 46 states that a certificate issued under the common seal specifying shares held by a person shall be PRIMA FACIE evidence of title — not conclusive proof.","Ebook","Other options: Prima facie evidence is NOT conclusive — it can be rebutted · Certificate of incorporation is conclusive evidence; share certificate is prima facie · Share certificates are issued under the company's common seal"],
[4,"A Private Company may commence business:",["Only after listing on a stock exchange","Only after filing a prospectus","Immediately after obtaining a certificate of incorporation","Only after getting a certificate of commencement of business like a public company"],2,"A Private Company may commence its business immediately after obtaining a certificate of incorporation. A Public Company additionally needs a 'Certificate of Commencement of Business.'","Ebook","Other options: Public company needs BOTH certificate of incorporation AND certificate of commencement · Private company does NOT need to file a prospectus · This is a key difference between private and public companies"],
[4,"The Object Clause of the Memorandum of Association:",["Describes the internal management rules","States the registered office location","Not only shows the objects for which the company is formed but also determines the extent of powers to achieve them","Lists the names of all founding shareholders"],2,"The Object Clause shows the objects for which the company is formed AND determines the extent of powers the company can exercise to achieve those objects — it is essential for public investors to know.","Ebook","Other options: Registered office clause states the state of incorporation — not objects · Internal management is covered by Articles of Association · Name clause covers the company name approval and requirements"],
[4,"Any act of the company that is ultra vires the Memorandum of Association is:",["Voidable by shareholders if ratified by majority","Valid if approved by the board","Wholly void and cannot be ratified even by all shareholders","Valid if done in good faith"],2,"Any act ultra vires the MOA is wholly VOID and cannot be ratified even by the whole body of shareholders — unlike ultra vires acts against the Articles (which can be ratified if intra vires MOA).","Ebook","Other options: Ultra vires MOA = permanently void; ultra vires AOA = can be ratified by shareholders · The MOA is supreme — AOA is subordinate to it · Good faith does not save an ultra vires act"],
[4,"According to Haney, a Joint Stock Company is defined as:",["A company created by royal charter","A voluntary association of individuals for profit, having capital divided into transferable shares, ownership of which is condition of membership","A government enterprise with common stock","An association of banks for collective investment"],1,"Haney defined a Joint Stock Company as 'a voluntary association of individuals for profit, having a capital divided into transferable shares. The ownership of which is the condition of membership.'","Ebook","Other options: Haney emphasizes VOLUNTARY, FOR PROFIT, TRANSFERABLE SHARES, MEMBERSHIP condition · Royal charter companies are Chartered Companies, not Haney's definition · Lord Lindley's definition focused on contribution to common stock and profit/loss sharing"],
[4,"The managerial remuneration in a public company is restricted to a maximum of what percentage of net profits?",["5%","8%","11%","15%"],2,"Total managerial remuneration in a public company cannot exceed 11% of the net profits (Section 198). No such restriction applies to a private company.","Ebook","Other options: 11% is the ceiling for total managerial remuneration in a public company · Private companies have NO such restriction · This is a key difference between public and private company remuneration rules"],
[4,"What is the minimum quorum for a general meeting of a PUBLIC company (if Articles are silent)?",["2 members","3 members","5 members","7 members"],2,"If the Articles do not provide for a larger quorum, 5 members personally present constitute the quorum for a meeting of a public company. For a private company, it is 2.","Ebook","Other options: 2 = quorum for PRIVATE company meeting · 7 = minimum members to FORM a public company — not quorum · Quorum requirements can be increased by the Articles but not decreased below these minimums"],
[5,"Under Section 2(55) of the Companies Act, 2013, which of the following is a 'member' of a company?",["Any person who buys company products","A subscriber to the memorandum whose name is entered in register of members","A person who has applied for shares but allotment is pending","A bondholder whose bonds are listed on the exchange"],1,"Section 2(55) defines member as: subscriber to memorandum (entered in register), any person who agrees in writing to become member and whose name is entered, and any person whose name is entered as beneficial owner in depository records.","Ebook","Other options: Subscribers to memorandum are deemed members on registration — automatically · Pending allotment does not make a person a member · Bondholders/debenture holders are CREDITORS, not members"],
[5,"Can a minor become a member of a company under Indian law?",["Yes, through a guardian","No — a minor is incompetent to contract, so a contract with a minor is absolutely void","Yes, if shares are fully paid","Yes, with approval of a company court"],1,"Under Indian law, a minor is incompetent to contract. A contract with a minor is absolutely void. Therefore, a minor cannot become a member of a company.","Ebook","Other options: Even through a guardian, the minor cannot technically be a member — shares are void · In some jurisdictions guardians can hold shares for minors, but ICA makes minor contracts void · Fully paid shares do not overcome the legal incapacity of a minor"],
[5,"A Partnership Firm cannot hold shares in a company in its own name because:",["Partnership firms are prohibited by SEBI","A partnership firm has no legal personality","Partnership firms have unlimited liability","RBI regulations prevent it"],1,"A partnership firm has no legal personality — it cannot purchase shares in a company in its own name. However, individual partners can hold shares.","Ebook","Other options: A company CAN become member of another company — it has legal personality · Hindu Undivided Family can have shares in the name of the karta · Foreigners can become members with RBI/FERA permission"],
[5,"Which mode of acquiring membership involves the company issuing new shares through an IPO?",["By subscribing memorandum","By transmission","By allotment of shares","By estoppel"],2,"By allotment of shares — allotment occurs when the company issues shares initially either by way of Initial Public Offering (IPO) or Further Public Offering (FPO).","Ebook","Other options: Subscribing memorandum = at time of incorporation · Transmission = shares passing to legal heir on death · Estoppel = person entered in register who doesn't object becomes member by estoppel"],
[5,"A member ceases to be a member when shares are forfeited. Which of the following is ALSO a cause for cessation of membership?",["When a member takes a company loan","By redemption of redeemable preference shares","When a member attends an AGM","When a member nominates a person for directorship"],1,"Cessation of membership occurs by: transfer, sale under lien, forfeiture, surrender, death, insolvency, rescission for fraud, winding up, redemption of redeemable preference shares, and issue of share warrants.","Ebook","Other options: Taking a company loan does NOT end membership · Attending AGM is an exercise of membership rights, not cessation · Nominating directors is a shareholder right, not a cessation event"],
[5,"The liability of a member in a company limited by shares is:",["Unlimited — they must pay all company debts","Limited to the amount unpaid on their shares","Fixed guarantee amount payable only on winding up","Limited to their total investment including market value"],1,"In a company limited by shares, the liability of a member is limited to the amount (if any) unpaid on their shares. If shares are fully paid, liability is nil.","Ebook","Other options: Unlimited company = full liability for all debts · Limited by guarantee = fixed guarantee amount on winding up · Market value is irrelevant — only FACE VALUE matters for liability"],
[5,"Under Section 179(3)(c) and (d) of the Companies Act, 2013, the power to borrow money can only be exercised by directors:",["Through a verbal majority decision","By passing a resolution","Through a written letter to the registrar","By special approval from shareholders at every instance"],1,"Under Section 179(3)(c) and (d), directors have the power to pass a resolution to borrow money. The power to borrow can only be delegated by passing a resolution specifying the total borrowing limit.","Ebook","Other options: Verbal decisions are not valid for board powers under the Act · Shareholder approval at every instance would be impractical — resolution suffices for delegation · The resolution must specify the total amount that can be borrowed"],
[5,"Under Section 180, 'temporary loans' that are excluded from the borrowing restriction are defined as loans repayable:",["Within 3 months","Within 6 months from the date","Within 1 year","On demand within 2 years"],1,"According to Section 180, temporary loans are loans repayable on demand or within 6 months from the date — including short-term, cash credit arrangements, and discounting of bills.","Ebook","Other options: 3 months is a common distractor but the Act specifies 6 months · 1 year applies to long-term; temporary is 6 months · Cash credit and bill discounting with banks are examples of temporary loans"],
[5,"When a company borrows beyond its authority (ultra vires borrowing), the consequence is:",["The loan is valid but at a higher interest rate","The contract is automatically void and lender cannot sue the company","The lender can only recover 50% of the amount","The borrowing is valid but directors are warned"],1,"Ultra vires borrowings are void. The contract is automatically void and the lender cannot sue the company. Securities given for such borrowings are also void and inoperative.","Ebook","Other options: The lender's only remedy is injunction (to prevent company from using the money) or subrogation · Securities for ultra vires borrowings are void — not enforceable · Directors attract PERSONAL liability for ultra vires borrowings"],
[5,"'Subrogation' in the context of ultra vires borrowings means:",["The lender forfeits his right to recover","One individual/group is substituted in respect of a debt — lender can sue company for money actually used for intra vires purposes","The government takes over the borrowing obligation","The court automatically repays the lender"],1,"Subrogation: when one party is substituted for another in respect of a debt. The lender can sue the company for the portion of the ultra vires loan that was actually applied towards intra vires (valid) purposes.","Ebook","Other options: Subrogation only applies to the extent money was used for valid purposes · The lender CANNOT recover for the full ultra vires borrowing via normal suit · Injunction is a separate remedy — to stop the company from using the money"],
[5,"'Syndicated borrowing' is where:",["A company borrows from one bank only","A company borrows from multiple lenders under one agreement covering the whole group","Government borrows from private sector","A company issues shares instead of taking loans"],1,"Syndicated borrowing: when a borrower requires a large fund, a group of lenders provides it under one agreement covering the whole group — rather than separate loans from each bank.","Ebook","Other options: Bilateral borrowing = borrowing from ONE financial institution · Private borrowing = company takes loan from banks/private institutions · Syndicated lending reduces individual lender risk"],
[5,"The rule in Foss v. Harbottle established two key principles. Which pair is correct?",["The company can sue only through its members; minority always wins","Proper plaintiff rule (company sues for wrong done to it) and majority rule principle (courts don't interfere if majority can ratify)","Minority shareholders can always sue; majority decisions are binding on directors only","The court always interferes in internal company disputes"],1,"Foss v. Harbottle established: (1) Proper plaintiff rule — a wrong to the company is for the COMPANY to sue (not individual members); (2) Majority rule — if the wrong can be ratified by majority, courts won't interfere.","Ebook","Other options: The case was dismissed — so minority shareholders did NOT win · Minority shareholders can sue only under the four exceptions to the rule · The rule protects majority decisions — courts respect corporate democracy"],
[5,"Which of the following is an exception to the rule in Foss v. Harbottle?",["When dividends are delayed","When a resolution is passed unanimously","When there is fraud on the minority","When the company changes its registered office"],2,"The four exceptions to Foss v. Harbottle are: (1) Ultra vires and illegality, (2) Actions requiring special majority, (3) Invasion of individual rights, (4) FRAUD ON THE MINORITY.","Ebook","Other options: Dividend delay is a member's right issue but not an exception to Foss v. Harbottle · Unanimous resolutions don't trigger exceptions — they are valid majority acts · Registered office change is an ordinary corporate act"],
[5,"Winding up of a company by court can be ordered if members fall below the minimum. The minimum for a PUBLIC company before court-ordered winding up applies is:",["2","5","7","10"],2,"A public company may be wound up if its members are reduced below seven. In case of a private company, it is below two.","Ebook","Other options: 2 = minimum for PRIVATE company before winding up · 7 = minimum members for PUBLIC company — if reduced below 7, court can wind up · This is distinct from quorum (5) and formation minimum (7)"],
[5,"In Members' Voluntary Winding Up, the directors must submit a statutory declaration stating the company:",["Has achieved its business objectives","Is ready to be listed on a new exchange","Has no debts or is able to pay debts within one year from commencement of winding up","Will merge with another company"],2,"In members' voluntary winding up, the majority of directors resolve to wind up and submit a statutory declaration (verified by the company's auditor) that the company has no debts OR is able to pay its debts in full within one year.","Ebook","Other options: No debts OR ability to pay within one year — either condition suffices · The declaration is verified by the AUDITOR, not just by directors alone · Creditors' voluntary winding up applies when company is insolvent"],
[5,"The Consumer Protection Act, 1986 was enacted to provide:",["Protection only to industrial buyers","A simpler and quicker redressal to consumer grievances, promoting and protecting consumer interests","Protection only for goods, not services","Exclusive courts for multinational company disputes"],1,"The Consumer Protection Act, 1986 was enacted to provide a simpler and quicker redressal to consumer grievances, and to promote and protect consumer interests against deficiencies and defects in goods or services.","Ebook","Other options: The Act covers BOTH goods AND services · It covers all sectors: private, public, and cooperative · Defects in goods for COMMERCIAL purposes are NOT covered — only personal use"],
[5,"Under the Consumer Protection Act, claims between Rs 5 lakh and Rs 20 lakh should be filed with:",["District forum","State Commission","National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission","Supreme Court"],1,"Claims of less than Rs 5 lakh → District Forum; Rs 5 lakh to Rs 20 lakh → State Commission; More than Rs 20 lakh → National Commission directly.","Ebook","Other options: District forum handles <Rs 5 lakh claims · National Commission handles >Rs 20 lakh · The hierarchy: District → State → National → Supreme Court on appeal"],
[5,"Under the Consumer Protection Act, complaints must be filed within:",["6 months of buying the product","1 year of buying the product","2 years of buying the product or using the service","5 years — consistent with contract law"],2,"Complaints must be filed within TWO YEARS of buying the product or using the service.","Ebook","Other options: 6 months is not the limitation period · 1 year is the winding up delay ground, not consumer complaint limitation · 5 years is a general limitation period under Limitation Act — not for consumer complaints"],
[5,"Under the Consumer Protection Act, if a defaulter does not appear despite notices, the consumer court can sentence him to a maximum imprisonment of:",["1 year","2 years","3 years","5 years"],2,"Consumer courts can sentence a defaulter to a maximum of THREE YEARS' imprisonment and impose a fine of Rs 10,000.","Ebook","Other options: 3 years imprisonment + Rs 10,000 fine is the maximum penalty · Courts can issue warrants and use police to enforce orders · The fine amount is Rs 10,000 specifically — not Rs 1 lakh"],
[5,"The Information Technology Act, 2000 was notified on:",["1 January 2000","15 August 2000","17 October 2000","26 January 2001"],2,"The Information Technology Act, 2000 was notified on 17 October 2000, enacted after the adoption of the LPG policy in 1991 as India grew in IT and e-commerce.","Ebook","Other options: The year is 2000 but the date is 17 October — not January or August · IT Act established Cyber Appellate Tribunal for cyber crime disputes · The first-ever cybercrime was recorded in 1820 according to the ebook"],
[5,"The Right to Information (RTI) Act, 2005 empowered which authority as the highest authority to order any office to provide information?",["The Supreme Court of India","The Prime Minister's Office","The Information Commission","The Comptroller and Auditor General"],2,"The RTI Act deliberately empowered the Information Commission as the highest authority in the country with the mandate to order any office to provide information and to fine officials who do not comply.","Ebook","Other options: The Information Commission was empowered — not the judiciary · The RTI Act didn't create a new bureaucracy — it mandated existing officials to be transparent · CAG is an auditing authority — not the RTI enforcement authority"],
[5,"Books of accounts under Section 128 of the Companies Act, 2013 must be retained for:",["3 financial years","5 financial years","8 financial years","10 financial years"],2,"Under Section 128, the company shall retain books of accounts with relevant vouchers and financial records for a period of 8 financial years.","Ebook","Other options: 5 years is a common distractor but the Companies Act specifies 8 years · Books must be kept on accrual basis and double entry system · Books can be maintained in electronic mode"]
  ]
};
